    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • 1971 Nissan Skyline 2000GT-R Is Now an 833-Piece Mattel Brick Shop Set That Can Be Customized\nHonda's Tire Repair Kit Recall Is Back, Supplier Mistakenly Shipped Bad Replacements\n12 photos\nPhoto: Honda\nAmerican Honda has filed a safety recall with the federal watchdog to repair a previous remedy. Known as 26V663 in the NHTSA's vernacular, the campaign is estimated to include 4,800-odd units of the CR-V Hybrid and Accord Hybrid in the United States of America, namely vehicles that were previously remedied under 26V366.\nThe previous campaign went public in June 2026, comprising a staggering 1,049,883 vehicles in the United States of America. Beyond the CR-V Hybrid and Accord Hybrid, the original safety recall also included the fuel-cell version of the RAV4 's biggest rival. Tire repair kits handed over as remedy were assembled around the same flawed parts the summer campaign was supposed to clear out.\nAmerican Honda's fix was to swap the kit's nozzle, and nothing more. But in the words of the automaker's recall filing, the supplier failed to separate old nozzles from the new ones. Troy, Michigan-based TEK Corporation is the supplier of both the suspect and the remedy kits.\nAs implied, the kit contains a nozzle that needs to be threaded onto the valve stem. The compressor is then switched on, allowing sealant to flow in to fix the puncture. Said nozzle carries a one-way valve that keeps everything moving toward the tire, not back into the kit's bottle. Connecting it wrong, pressure builds into the sealant bottle instead.\nA relief valve does exist to bleed that off, but if it fails while the nozzle is seated wrong, the cap ultimately detaches. American Honda claims the cap can strike the user or anyone standing nearby, increasing the risk of injury. Not exactly the most pleasant experience, for sure, especially if you're kneeling on a highway shoulder at night with a flat tire.\nPhoto: Honda Per the automaker's report, American Honda counted zero injuries potentially related to the defect so far. 2,745 units of the CR-V Hybrid represent the biggest chunk of the affected population, with production dates ranging from September 8, 2022, through May 20, 2026.\nThe Accord Hybrid is listed with 2,058 units from January 6, 2023, to the same May 2026 cutoff. The collector's item is a single unit of the 2025 model year CR-V e:FCEV. Best described as a plug-in hydrogen fuel-cell electric crossover, the CR-V e:FCEV is exclusive to California.\nA lease-only affair, the fuel-cell specification is exclusive to Calif because that's the only state with a proper hydrogen infrastructure for passenger vehicles. Hand-built in small batches in Marysville, Ohio, the CR-V e:FCEV packs two high-pressure hydrogen tanks and a 17.7-kWh traction battery. Said battery makes the design unusual, giving the CR-V e:FCEV Level 2 and Level 1 charging capability.\nThe subject unit was produced on December 9, 2024. The mix-up condition surfaced on July 3, 2026, when a Honda dealership reported the wrong countermeasure parts. A joint inventory dig with TEK followed, enabling America Honda to track down which vehicles may have received the wrong remedy tire repair kits.\nAffected VINs will become searchable on the automaker's website and the NHTSA's recall portal on October 17, 2026. Dealer notifications have been sent, while customer notification letters should be mailed in November 2026.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nHonda honda cr-v Honda Accord Hybrid recall\nDownload: Honda CR-V and Accord hybrid tire sealant bottle cap recall (PDF)\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT \"Every Old Car Has Rust\": Owner Sells 1958 Chevrolet Impala Parked for Too Many Years",
